# SYNOPSIS

```text
region = header (4096) strings rings
ring   = trace header (128) one ring of 40-byte slots
slot   = sequence (u64) record (32)
```

# DESCRIPTION

A traced process records what it does into a region of memory.
vx-trace(1) and the other readers read it back, alone or merged with the kernel's records into one timeline.
This is version 2.
All integers are little-endian; offsets are from the start of what is named.

@node record The record

Every record is 32 bytes:

| Offset | Size | Field | Meaning |
| 0 | 8 | `time` | the raw cycle counter (TSC, `CNTVCT_EL0`, rv64 `time`), ticking at `counter_hz`, not nanoseconds |
| 8 | 2 | `kind` | what the record is |
| 10 | 2 | `cpu` | the CPU where known, else `0xffff` |
| 12 | 4 | `tid` | the thread: the process number shifted left 12 bits, or the thread index |
| 16 | 8 | `a` | by kind |
| 24 | 8 | `b` | by kind |

Kinds `0x0000` to `0x3fff` are the kernel's categories, `0x4000` to `0x7fff` user space's, and `0x8000` to `0xffff` are reserved.
User kinds:

| Kind | Name | `time` | `a` | `b` |
| `0x4001` | MARK | when | first 8 bytes of a label | the next 8 |
| `0x4002` | ZONE | start | end, in ticks | name id |
| `0x4003` | FLOW | when | flow id | name id |
| `0x4004` | COUNTER | when | name id | the value, two's complement |

A zone is written once, when it ends, so a crash loses only the zones still open; zones on a thread nest by time.
A mark's label is UTF-8, NUL padded, at most 16 bytes.
A flow joins records across threads and processes.

The kernel's channel records, in its IPC category, are kind 80 (channel write; `a` the flow id, `b` the bytes in the low 32 bits and the handles above), 81 (channel read, the same) and 82 (donation: `a` the flow id, `b` the caller's trace id).

## Flow ids for IPC

Both ends of an IPC call compute the flow id from what they share, so nothing travels with the message.
Let `channel_id` be the smaller of the channel's koid and its peer's, and `txid` the message's first 4 bytes (0 if it is shorter).
In 64-bit wrapping arithmetic:

```text
z = channel_id * 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15 + txid
z = (z ^ (z >> 30)) * 0xBF58476D1CE4E5B9
z = (z ^ (z >> 27)) * 0x94D049BB133111EB
flow = z ^ (z >> 31)
```

A call and its reply share one flow, because the reply echoes the transaction id (wire(6)).
A ring uses the same function with the ring VMO's koid and the slot's sequence number.

@node region The region

A region begins with a 4096-byte header:

| Offset | Size | Field |
| 0 | 4 | magic, `"tdtr"` (0x72746474) |
| 4 | 4 | version, 2 |
| 8 | 8 | `counter_hz`, the counter's frequency |
| 16 | 8 | `start`, the counter when recording began |
| 24 | 4 | process id |
| 28 | 4 | ring count |
| 32 | 8 | ring size in bytes, header included |
| 40 | 8 | the string table's offset |
| 48 | 8 | the string table's size |
| 56 | 8 | bytes of the string table used (atomic) |
| 64 | 8 | the first ring's offset |
| 72 | 4 | rings claimed so far (atomic) |
| 76 | 4 | flags: bit 0, circular; else one-shot |
| 80 | 8 | the categories enabled |
| 88 | 4008 | reserved, zero |

## The string table

Names are entries of a 4-byte length, then the UTF-8 bytes, padded to a multiple of 4.
A name's id is its entry's offset in the table.
Entries are appended and never change.
A reader that meets an id with no entry shows `#<id>`.

## Rings

Rings follow, one for each thread that has written; a thread claims the next ring when it first writes.
A ring is a 128-byte trace header, then a one ring (the format of `lib/ring`, magic `"VRNG"`) in the rest of the ring's bytes.
The kernel's rings, one for each CPU, are the same.
The trace header:

| Offset | Size | Field |
| 0 | 8 | `frequency`: the counter's Hz |
| 8 | 8 | `session`: the region's `start` |
| 16 | 8 | records dropped (one-shot, when full) |
| 24 | 8 | time of the first drop |
| 32 | 8 | time of the last drop |
| 40 | 4 | mode: 0 one-shot, 1 circular |
| 44 | 4 | cpu: `0xffffffff` for a thread's ring |
| 48 | 4 | magic, `"ring"` (0x676e6972) |
| 52 | 4 | the thread's `tid`, 0 for a CPU's ring |
| 56 | 72 | reserved, zero |

The one ring is an overwrite ring of 40-byte slots with the format descriptor `"TRC2"` (0x32435254).
Each slot is the ring's u64 sequence, then a record.
The slot count is a power of two, as many as fit.
Only the owning thread or CPU writes, without a lock: it sets the slot's sequence to all ones, writes the record, sets the sequence to the slot's cursor, and publishes its cursor.
A circular ring overwrites its oldest records; a one-shot ring stops when the cursor reaches the slot count, and counts what it drops in the header.

A reader keeps its own cursor.
It starts at the writer's cursor less the slot count (0 if fewer were written), keeps a slot only if its sequence equalled its cursor before and after the copy, and counts the rest as dropped.
A reader skips a ring whose magic is not `"ring"`, and merges all the rings' records by `time`.

## Categories

The categories enabled are a 64-bit mask, and a disabled trace point costs one load and one branch.
The user categories are bit 0 `app`, 1 `frame`, 2 `audio`, 3 `input`, 4 `ipc`, 5 `io` and 6 `mark`; the rest are reserved.

@node export Files and the console

A recording on the host is a file, `<pid>.trace`, in the directory the environment variable `TODHCHAI_TRACE` names, mapped shared so that it survives the process.
`TODHCHAI_TRACE_CATEGORIES` (a comma separated list of the names above) and `TODHCHAI_TRACE_CIRCULAR` (set for circular) choose what and how.

On croi a trace session makes the region and gives the process a VMO of it.
Its process id is `0xF0000` plus its number in the session.
When it ends, the session writes each region compacted (the strings used, and each ring's records in order from sequence 0) and the kernel's rings as `kernel.trace` (process 0, a ring a CPU, no strings).
The files leave through the debuglog, in lines:

```text
td-trace NAME OFFSET BASE64
td-trace NAME OFFSET zero COUNT
td-trace NAME end SIZE
```

A BASE64 line carries up to 120 bytes at OFFSET; a `zero` line a run of COUNT zero bytes at OFFSET, used for runs of 120 or more; the `end` line gives the file's size.
Lines are paced to the console's rate, and the debuglog drops records if its console falls behind, which `vx trace collect` reports as a missing piece.

